WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.830
- I hum a lot of things to myself

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.960
looking like a crazy person. But that's-

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.390
- I'm usually humming with a phone kind of

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.590
videoing myself, humming.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.953
- That was it.

00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:10.140
So I, the Captain Marvel
theme, I was actually out

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.060
on a walk and I came up with the theme

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.090
and sang it on my phone,
and it's still on my phone.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.740
And that is the Captain Marvel theme.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.823
(gentle music plays)

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.049
Hi, Natalie.

00:00:29.049 --> 00:00:29.882
Hello. How are you?

00:00:29.882 --> 00:00:30.810
- Yeah, great, thank you.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.940
- What did you do when you
found out you were accepted?

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.080
- Oh, I was driving along
in the car with my family.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.660
I was with my daughter
and my mom and dad and...

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.160
- Did she know what the Academy is?

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:41.993
Your daughter.

00:00:41.993 --> 00:00:43.470
- I think they vaguely knew what it was,

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.330
but they knew I was
excited about something

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.350
so that was nice.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.183
How about you?

00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:49.710
- It was the same, you
know, it's a pandemic time

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.130
so I was just home and I
found out, it was amazing.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.230
It's such a great honor to
be a part of this community

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.360
that we all loved and admired.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.870
I've been watching the Oscars
since I was six years old.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:01.980
I haven't missed one.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.710
I would wake up at three
in the morning in a stumble

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.940
and watch the Oscars.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.210
So for me, just being a
part of this community

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.647
and in some small way,
it's been a huge honor.

00:01:12.647 --> 00:01:16.397
(Captain Marvel theme plays)

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.290
So how did you know music was your thing?

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.900
- [Natalie] Well, I guess,
my mom was a music teacher.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.230
My grandma was a violinist,
so I've always been in music

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.630
but I think I, it was, I was
about five when I watched ET

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.430
and then I remember noticing
the score and hearing it

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.510
in my head afterwards.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.820
My sight reading was a bit
terrible, so I would always

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.740
make pe-, I'd learn something by ear

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.180
and then make some of it up.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.540
So I think that was my
foray into composing.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.848
- I still do that.
- Do you?

00:01:46.848 --> 00:01:49.110
(both laugh)

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.370
My teacher would be like, that's lovely.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.010
It's not what's on the
page, but. How about you?

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.080
When did you decide to
become a film composer?

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.610
- Don't think it was really a decision.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.280
I was five years old when
I started the conservatory.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.570
My dad was a violinist

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.510
but he had to give it up
to do anything but music.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.490
So kind of lived vicariously through me.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.890
And then I fell in love with films.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.460
I didn't really even
know the film scoring was

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.127
a job that people have done, you know?

00:02:14.127 --> 00:02:16.230
And as soon as I actually realized, wait,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.350
they're actually people
that get hired to write this

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.180
and this is their thing.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.680
I was really, really hooked.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.060
You're classically trained as well.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.760
Did you, do you ever feel
like you wanted to rebel

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.100
against just a classical repertoire?

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.230
Did you ever go through that
phase or was it just me?

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.063
- Oh, no, for sure.

00:02:32.063 --> 00:02:35.310
I remember going to music
college and I was learning

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.080
the violin and always had this feeling

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.290
that I kind of want to be
creating something a bit of,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.600
you know, I'm not just
playing somebody else's music

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.790
but I'm really grateful to
have had that experience

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.700
and I think it's so useful
to have sat in that position

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.640
and to have that kind of skillset.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.280
Yeah. I did a masters in film composing.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.930
I went to film school.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.620
Bringing that love of music
and film together as well,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.270
it's such a brilliant job.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.034
I'm so happy to be doing it.

00:03:02.034 --> 00:03:05.220
- I think it's so important to
kind of get to know the rules

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.330
so we can break them.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.100
That's what I always would say

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.590
because I think a lot of
the classical education

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.210
that we get when we are young, I mean,

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.300
I hated figured bass
and all of that stuff.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.100
I'm like, where am I
gonna reuse this stuff?

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.080
And then you start realizing, you know,

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.390
just in terms of voice
leading and, you know,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.220
a lot of the things that we do,

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.290
how it becomes automatic.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:30.066
It's like a emotional, an
intellectual muscle memory.

00:03:30.066 --> 00:03:34.290
(Pomp and Marmalade plays)

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.940
- [Narrator] Darkest Peru,

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.750
a vast unexplored wilderness
shrouded in history.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.690
- And how did you get into film music?

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.950
- I had an ex-boyfriend
who did a gap year working

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.660
at Abbey Road as a runner.
- Oh, cool.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.550
- [Natalie] So when I was
still at school and I would go

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.620
and see him and sneak in
the back of Abbey Road

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.780
and Trevor Jones,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.046
recording a League of
Extraordinary Gentlemen

00:03:57.046 --> 00:03:57.975
that was,
- Oh wow.

00:03:57.975 --> 00:04:00.180
- That was like watching
that being recorded.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.490
And I was just in there
all day, like, oh, wow.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.130
And then Trevor invited
us to the premiere.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:09.030
So seeing that kind of glitzy
side of Premiere as well,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.800
I think that got me completely hooked and.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.796
- Really? The premieres
are the part that I dread.

00:04:13.796 --> 00:04:16.230
I like everything else
before it in the premieres.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.880
I've been a musician my whole life

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.990
but what I really loved
was storytelling and films.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:23.910
I wanted to be a part of it so badly

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.440
because it was magic for me

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.440
just to see a film come
together from a speck

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.930
of an idea to this thing
that we sit and watch

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.840
with our popcorn, it's
like, it's incredible.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.268
I think it's pure magic,

00:04:35.268 --> 00:04:38.340
no matter the budget,
no matter the process.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.300
I think the fact that anything
that we watch gets made

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.980
is a miracle and music was
the language that I knew

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:47.970
that could be a part of,

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.580
that could be my
contribution to filmmaking.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.413
- (indistinct)

00:04:51.413 --> 00:04:52.246
- I can't.
- Yes, you can.

00:04:52.246 --> 00:04:55.067
Actually you just say
here and you throw it out.

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:58.380
- Right. And then I'm sure
they'll happily let us go.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.668
How do they keep finding us?

00:04:59.668 --> 00:05:01.320
- You're basically a walking disco ball.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.100
- Oh, I've got really interested
in standup comedy recently.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.600
I'm like reading Judd Apatow's book.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.490
He was talking about how's like this pain

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.710
at the beginning of trying
to start the idea off.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.410
And then after a certain point
it starts writing itself.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.510
And it's like, it's always the beginning

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.760
for me that's, it's
like, I've got nothing.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.353
I'm staring at a blank page.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:21.780
When it all starts coming
together and you've got into

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.300
the project and it's, you're
watching and you're like, oh,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.590
I know that should be that theme.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.440
And I love that moment
where you've kind of lined

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.190
the pieces up and it
feels like it's easier.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.150
I don't know about you.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.270
- You need to like the pain
of creation to do what we do,

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:37.320
I think, right?

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.010
For me, a lot of times
it's just right before it.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.470
That's like for Captain
Marvel, I remember I was

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.350
in the studio for two days
and 'cause it was already

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.120
announced that I was
scoring, and so I was getting

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.710
all these messages from people saying,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.510
oh, this theme better be good.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.440
It should be timeless.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.290
It should be so this,
it should be so that.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:55.980
So I'm like, thank you for the pressure.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.040
So I remember sitting in
the studio for two days just

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.980
trying out different things
and not being happy with it.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.130
So in those situations I
actually get out of the studio

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.260
and I go for a walk.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.990
There's something about writing a theme,

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.030
just humming the theme.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.800
If you're need a tune, a
proper theme for something,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:17.880
I need to be able to sing it.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.550
- Paul McCartney was saying
for coming up with songs

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.400
that if you hum something,
you come up with an idea

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.530
and you can remember it the next day.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.090
So you kind of sleep on it as well.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.160
I think if it's got
that sort of durability

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.830
that it sticks in your
head for a long time.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.110
'Cause sometimes you can go
back and record something.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.540
And I think if it also
sticks in your head,

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.250
it's kind of a good test for it as well.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:39.083
If that makes any sense.
- Yeah.

00:06:39.083 --> 00:06:41.340
I think the next morning test
is a really important one.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.860
- Yeah, there's a trombone group

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:48.000
and my friend filmed them
playing the Loki theme.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.280
And it's just like, that was
that was something I hummed

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.380
into my phone.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.560
And now there's, people
associate it with this character

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.750
and yeah's they want to
play it in a school band.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.790
- Once you hit the school
band you know you made it.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.410
You know, it's really what it is.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.400
That's the (laughs).

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.360
- It's just amazing.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.563
It's so satisfying.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.611
(energetic music plays)

00:07:08.611 --> 00:07:10.510
- (whimpers)

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.593
(grunts)

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.090
- So as composers, we have to
wear different hats sometimes

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.220
and we're the musician, we're the writer

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.180
and we're the producer.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.640
And can you talk a little
bit about the different roles

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:24.990
that we have?

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.970
- [Natalie] I kind of enjoy
all the different parts

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.210
of it equally, but I
never enjoy finishing.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.700
I always feel like I want
to keep tweaking this

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:33.750
or maybe I could add some more.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:37.410
I find it really hard to kind
of reach that cutoff point

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:41.850
where you have to deliver a
cue and stop mixing something.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.430
I think that's the worst
part for me, is letting go.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.740
- Yeah. Letting go. And knowing
when it's enough, right?

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.480
I think that's the key.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.120
Like when do you stop adding more?

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.200
Yeah, I think that's a pretty
important part of the process.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.650
And I think a lot of times,
deadlines really help with that

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.870
because when you know you
have to finish something

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:04.680
by the next day, you
only have that much room

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:05.700
to play around with it.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.053
So that really helps.

00:08:07.893 --> 00:08:09.671
- (shouts)
(water splashing)

00:08:09.671 --> 00:08:12.588
(calm theme plays)

00:08:21.261 --> 00:08:23.428
(oinking)

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:27.960
- If you were to go back to
yes, there's a time machine

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.800
and you saw yourself, what would
you tell your younger self?

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:36.800
- I definitely think when I
left film school in early 2000s,

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:42.090
there was a real lack of women
in the industry composing.

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.383
I didn't really feel like
I had a place, I kind of,

00:08:44.383 --> 00:08:47.130
I was made to feel like
a second class citizen

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:48.390
at that point in time.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.000
I went back to playing
the violin professionally

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.370
which in hindsight was
brilliant because, you know,

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.890
sitting in the orchestra,
it gives you a lot of skills

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.080
and tools and orchestration ideas.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.040
But I wish I'd carried on and, you know,

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.440
had some perseverance because, you know,

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.480
we are proving that
there is a space for us.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.420
And I think a few years later it,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.060
it's really opened up and the
industry's really changing.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.740
- Absolutely.
- Yeah. I don't know.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.780
What would you say to your younger self?

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.310
- I'd say don't worry too much.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:18.603
It's all gonna be okay.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.650
I, it's still an advice
to myself currently.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:26.100
I would worry less and
just keep with the flow.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.780
- Yeah. That sounds like good advice

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.040
just generally for everyone.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.690
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:31.920
This has been so great.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.990
It's lovely finally meeting you in person.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.000
It's just been such a
pleasure getting to meet

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:36.833
and chat with you.

00:09:36.833 --> 00:09:37.666
- Oh, thank you.

00:09:37.666 --> 00:09:39.510
It's so exciting to join the academy

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:40.740
and get to meet you.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.650
It's just so many exciting
things all at once.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.453
- Oh, and more to come.

00:09:45.453 --> 00:09:46.642
- For you too.

00:09:46.642 --> 00:09:49.725
(upbeat music plays)

